# This repo has a long-standing CRLF-vs-LF drift problem: several files
# carry CRLF in the working tree but LF in git HEAD (or vice versa),
# so touching them at all makes `git diff`/`git status` show the whole
# file as changed even when only one real line was edited. There was no
# .gitattributes at all before this, so git had no rule to normalize
# line endings on checkin - whatever the editor/OS/checkout happened to
# produce is what got committed.
#
# This only affects files as they're next added/committed - it does NOT
# retroactively rewrite anything already in the working tree or git
# history. Existing drifted files stay as-is until they're next edited
# and re-added; from that point on they'll normalize to LF instead of
# drifting again. A one-time `git add --renormalize .` would fix
# everything immediately, but that touches nearly every file in the repo
# in one commit - left for whoever owns the repo to decide on, not done
# here.

* text=auto eol=lf

# Explicit text types (belt-and-suspenders on top of text=auto's
# content-based detection).
*.php text
*.js text
*.css text
*.scss text
*.html text
*.htm text
*.json text
*.md text
*.txt text
*.xml text
*.svg text
*.yml text
*.yaml text
.gitattributes text
.gitignore text
.nvmrc text

# Windows-specific scripts, if any ever get added, should keep CRLF.
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf

# Binary assets - never touch these, line-ending normalization on a
# binary file corrupts it.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.webp binary
*.ico binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf binary
*.otf binary
*.eot binary
*.pdf binary
*.zip binary
*.mp4 binary
*.mov binary
*.gz binary
